"1\n00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:04,229\nThe Learning Brain. Series 3: Generative AI\n\n2\n00:00:06,100 --> 00:00:07,235\nEpisode 1\n\n3\n00:00:07,315 --> 00:00:09,378\nPrediction machines\n\n4\n00:00:12,178 --> 00:00:18,008\nNine people listened to a thirty minute podcast while electrodes recorded from their brains.\n\n5\n00:00:18,415 --> 00:00:22,061\nTheir brains were predicting each next word before it arrived,\n\n6\n00:00:22,287 --> 00:00:25,208\nand reacting with surprise when the guess was wrong.\n\n7\n00:00:25,773 --> 00:00:29,629\nA language model listening to the same story did the same.\n\n8\n00:00:29,799 --> 00:00:32,411\nThe study found three shared principles.\n\n9\n00:00:32,746 --> 00:00:35,155\nPredict the next word before it appears.\n\n10\n00:00:35,508 --> 00:00:38,363\nCompare that prediction with what actually comes.\n\n11\n00:00:38,650 --> 00:00:41,156\nAnd represent each word in its context.\n\n12\n00:00:41,326 --> 00:00:49,603\nAcross many models, the ones that were better at predicting the next word were also better at predicting brain responses to sentences.\n\n13\n00:00:50,048 --> 00:00:54,230\nThe strongest captured nearly all of the explainable variance.\n\n14\n00:00:54,400 --> 00:00:56,189\nBut the learning is very different.\n\n15\n00:00:56,700 --> 00:00:59,963\nChildren become fluent on tens of millions of words,\n\n16\n00:01:00,200 --> 00:01:02,434\nheard in a social and physical world.\n\n17\n00:01:03,031 --> 00:01:05,643\nLarge models are trained on trillions,\n\n18\n00:01:05,841 --> 00:01:08,854\naround four or five orders of magnitude more.\n\n19\n00:01:09,024 --> 00:01:11,623\nAnd prediction is not the whole of cognition.\n\n20\n00:01:12,043 --> 00:01:13,675\nA model has no body,\n\n21\n00:01:13,806 --> 00:01:15,004\nno goals of its own,\n\n22\n00:01:15,024 --> 00:01:17,828\nand no memory of you unless one is built in.\n\n23\n00:01:18,279 --> 00:01:20,259\nIt predicts what text is likely,\n\n24\n00:01:20,479 --> 00:01:21,707\nnot what is true.\n\n25\n00:01:21,877 --> 00:01:22,680\nA caution.\n\n26\n00:01:23,040 --> 00:01:26,294\nSimilar computations do not mean similar minds.\n\n27\n00:01:26,669 --> 00:01:29,166\nThese studies compare language processing,\n\n28\n00:01:29,367 --> 00:01:32,842\nmostly listening, in a handful of people and models.\n\n29\n00:01:33,012 --> 00:01:33,840\nFor teaching.\n\n30\n00:01:34,246 --> 00:01:37,402\nExplain AI to students as a prediction engine,\n\n31\n00:01:37,468 --> 00:01:39,457\nso they expect fluent errors.\n\n32\n00:01:39,858 --> 00:01:44,651\nUse prediction in your own teaching: ask students to guess before they read,\n\n33\n00:01:44,752 --> 00:01:47,297\nbecause a wrong guess sharpens attention.\n\n34\n00:01:47,680 --> 00:01:51,251\nAnd remind them that their learning runs on effort and meaning,\n\n35\n00:01:51,373 --> 00:01:52,520\nnot volume.\n\n36\n00:01:52,690 --> 00:01:54,901\nBrains and models both predict.\n\n37\n00:01:55,204 --> 00:01:57,463\nOnly one of them is trying to understand.\n\n"
